{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EG1d5xxdEJ77DUKbZDcg93","type":"tool_result","content":"20: - `lib.codec` / `lib.warmups_codecs` (D25 re-measure): 341 + 88 regexes compiled at import = ~0.2 s of a 0.73 s sequential single. Compile lazily (e.g. a `functools.cache`d `_re(name)` accessor) -- it is now the biggest fixed cost of every script-lane solver.\n21: - `lib.codec` import is ~0.3 s, almost all of it from ~340 module-level `re.compile`s (cProfile, D24 single). Compiling lazily would save that on every script-lane solver start.\n22: - Solver skeleton: when `group_by_set` gives one group, split the questions by artifact across forked workers (independent members), like D24 solve.py `main()`.\n23: - `lib.common.gm_error_body(artdir) -> bytes|None`: detect a GM error JSON (`rate_limited`, ...) saved in place of an\n24:   artifact, so every solver fails loudly. Better still: the harness download step (hctf/hb_timed.py) should retry on\n25:   `{\"error\":\"rate_limited\"}` and never save it as the artifact. That would have saved this timed attempt.\n26: - `lib.common.iter_entries(root, leaf_ext=...)`: don't load a member just to sniff for a nested zip when its name has a script/text extension (.js/.ps1/.py/.vbs/.cmd/.bat/.sh/.mjs/.php/...). Today it decrypts every AES member of a Mastery bundle up front (375 PBKDF2 = ~0.2 s locally, many times that on Homebase), and each forked set repeats it. See `_iter_entries` in this solve.py.\n27: - `lib.common._zip_open`: prefetch WinZip-AES keys for all members in parallel (salt = bytes after the local header; `hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac` in a ThreadPoolExecutor), as D23 solve.py `_prefetch_keys` does. Every script-lane Mastery solver pays ~1 ms x members serially today.\n28: - `lib.codec`: ~295 module-level `re.compile`s cost ~0.3 s per solver start, and `_find_codecs_uncached` runs ~16 regex probes per text (4669 searches / 287 texts = 0.4 s on the D23 Mastery). Compile lazily and gate each detector on a cheap substring test (`'Codec_' in text`, ...).\n29: - `lib.codec.find_codecs`: try the direct `_CODEC_RE` + `material_text` + `decode` reading first and return early on a plan, before the carrier/C#-fold/peel fallbacks (each script-lane solver pays ~27 ms per member for them otherwise). Also: `peel_plan`'s wall-clock `budget` makes results depend on host load.\n30: \n31: VARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D27 speed cycle): the single took 2-5.8 s because `analyse_node` (.mjs/.cjs members, 8 per single) ran the full `codec.find_codecs` peel scan (~170 ms/file, `_awksed_codecs`/`peel_plan`). Parse rule: decode the declared `Codec_<id>` + `Material_<id>` pair directly first (same fast path as `_codec_hint`) and fall back to `find_codecs` only when that yields no `steps=` plan -> single 0.7 s, all answers unchanged (52/52 cases pass; 7432c509 still unverifiable, 3075/3075 filled).\n32: \n33: ## Proposed lib additions (D27 speed)\n34: - `lib.codec.find_codecs`: try the direct `_CODEC_RE` spec + `material_text` + `decode` path before `_awksed_codecs` (the peel scan dominates script-lane solver time whenever a solver calls find_codecs unconditionally).\n35: \n36: VARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D28 speed re-flag: \"7.99 s single\" measured under host load, loadavg 8.5-10 with 3 parallel generalize/duel workers). Direct `solve.py` on the single = 0.6-0.9 s wall (1703/1703), cProfile 1.16 s (0.33 s imports, mostly `lib.codec` regex compiles). `hctf solver-run` took 47 s, but 76 s of a timed probe was `hctf.solvers.dispatch` (re-reads all 52 case contracts + `question_index()` + difflib per question), NOT the solver: `run_solver` on the full contract = 0.69 s. No solver change; 51/52 cases pass, 7432c509 is still unverifiable (expected.json {}), 3075/3075 \n37: \n38: ## Proposed lib additions (D28 speed)\n39: - `hctf.solvers.dispatch`: cache the per-solver `own` question set (json-load of every `cases/*/contract.json`, 1-20 MB each x 52) keyed by mtime, and skip 